# 4. Discussion

The Pauli Exclusion Principle is an almost direct consequence of the spin-statistics connection which was first proved by Pauli in 1940<sup>14</sup>. Pauli's proof is not easy to follow, and it was soon followed by other arguments that tried to give the theorem a more obvious basis. In particular the paper by Lüders and Zumino<sup>15</sup> states very clearly the postulates that must be fulfilled by a field theory for the spin-statistics connection to hold:

- the theory is invariant with respect to the proper inhomogeneous Lorentz group (this includes translations, but does not include reflections);
- two operators of the same field at points separated by a spacelike interval either commute or anticommute;
- the vacuum is the state of lowest energy;
- the metric of the Hilbert space is positive definite;
- the vacuum is not identically annihilated by a field.

